#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124212 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/id #uuid "1a855d24-cb78-5398-89e1-3bfda6907f36" :question/math-academy-id "e-4787" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients whose degree is less than or equal to $2$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. For any $p(x),q(x) \\in L$, we have $p(x)+q(x) = q(x)+p(x)$.\n2. For any $p(x),q(x),r(x) \\in L$, we have $[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x) = p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)]$.\n3. For each $p(x) \\in L$, we have $-p(x) \\in L$ such that $p(x)+(-p(x))=0$." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x), q(x) \\in L$, we obtain that\n$p(x) + q(x)= q(x) +p(x)$\nsince polynomial addition is commutative.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x),r(x) \\in L$, we obtain that\n$[p(x) + q(x)] + r(x) = p(x) + [q(x) + r(x)]$\nsince polynomial addition is associative.\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any polynomial $p(x) \\in L$, there is a corresponding negative polynomial $-p(x)$ such that\n$p(x) + (-p(x))= p(x) - p(x) = 0$.\nNotice that\n$\\deg(-p(x)) = \\deg(p(x)) \\leq 2$,\nhence $-p(x) \\in L$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124318 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/id #uuid "57bf5fb6-02e9-56a1-9641-342123f45406" :question/math-academy-id "e-8229" :question/problem "Let $P$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices $A= (a_{ij})$ such that $a_{11} + a_{22} > 0$. Which of the following properties are true?\n\n1. For any $A \\in P$, we have $1 \\cdot A = A$.\n2. For any $A \\in P$ and $\\alpha,\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R}$, we have $\\alpha(\\beta A) = (\\alpha\\beta) A$.\n3. For any $A \\in P$ and $\\alpha,\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R}$, we have $(\\alpha+\\beta) A= \\alpha A + \\beta A$." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A = (a_{ij}) \\in P$, the entries are just real numbers. Thus, we have\n$1 \\cdot a_{ij} = a_{ij}$,\nand hence,\n$1\\cdot A=A$.\n- Statements II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A = (a_{ij}) \\in P$, the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha, \\beta \\in \\mathbb{R}$, we obtain that\n$\\alpha(\\beta a_{ij}) = (\\alpha\\beta) a_{ij}$,\nand hence,\n$\\alpha(\\beta A) = (\\alpha\\beta) A$.\n- Statements III is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A = (a_{ij}) \\in P$, the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha, \\beta \\in \\mathbb{R}$, we obtain that\n$(\\alpha+\\beta) a_{ij} = \\alpha a_{ij} + \\beta a_{ij}$,\nand hence,\n$(\\alpha+\\beta) A= \\alpha A + \\beta A$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124350 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/id #uuid "ff4fdeaf-bdb3-54d7-b5e9-2f3ad88b55a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9960" :question/problem "Let $M$ be the set of all $2\\times2$ real matrices whose entries are non-negative. Then, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $I_2 \\in M$\n2. For any $A \\in M$ and $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$, we have $\\alpha \\cdot A \\in M$.\n3. The set is closed under scalar multiplication." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ because all its entries are non-negative.\n- Statement II is false. There exists a $2\\times2$ real matrix in $M$ that has a scalar multiple not in $M$. For example, let $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\alpha=-1\\in\\mathbb{R}$. Notice that $A$ belongs to $M$ since statement I is true. However,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nSince the entries on the main diagonal are negative, we conclude that $-1 \\cdot A \\notin M$.\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4c52d5a-0844-5105-ab53-9eb50de6ede1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121130} {:answer/id #uuid "eb97c9b4-7896-56b3-be8d-9d8f9d2670e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121131} {:answer/id #uuid "ff605ad5-60a7-5783-9696-ad7ac1f6d9d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121132} {:answer/id #uuid "38dd97d7-614e-5024-b17b-a142e8e5b743" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121133} {:answer/id #uuid "3fefeb7d-9f86-58ee-8a04-8ba57c157f95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb97c9b4-7896-56b3-be8d-9d8f9d2670e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121131} :answer-field/id #uuid "97795195-e071-52ca-9410-8f360a4924cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121129}] :question/id #uuid "a98e5d99-323b-544b-bce7-36fa3d3ba9e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-106806" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the set of all $2 \\cdot 2$ real matrices with determinant $1$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. For any $A ∈ S$, we have $1 \\cdot A = A$.\n2. For any $A ∈ S$ and $α, β ∈ R$, we have $(α + β)A = αA + βA$.\n3. There is a matrix $A ∈ S$ such that $\\frac{1}{2}(2A) \\ne A$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125135 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e4246d4-50a8-53a3-8749-50c319245a90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123833} {:answer/id #uuid "cc783cdc-467b-59c1-a013-b84873924ce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123834} {:answer/id #uuid "db850734-49db-5064-9d06-6916c197afff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123835} {:answer/id #uuid "0209b75d-b7d6-5c0a-8a6b-1c79575d6ddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123836} {:answer/id #uuid "3873b058-c825-5962-9882-db067db051ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e4246d4-50a8-53a3-8749-50c319245a90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123833} :answer-field/id #uuid "28b11644-f447-57ec-aa80-12c48f1781ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123832}] :question/id #uuid "73f62269-52b9-5341-87d2-165ee03b0821" :question/math-academy-id "q-74700" :question/problem "Let $U$ be the set of polynomials of the form $ax^{3}$, where $a ∈ R$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The zero-polynomial belongs to $U$.\n2. For any $p(x), q(x) ∈ U$, we have $p(x) + q(x) = q(x) + p(x)$.\n3. For any $p(x), q(x), r(x) ∈ U$, we have $[p(x) - q(x)] - r(x) = p(x) - [q(x) - r(x)]$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125157 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94b3a172-3584-5ffd-982f-0f3370f6e593" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123965} {:answer/id #uuid "ac0af473-719f-51a5-a877-96dcd378fb16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123966} {:answer/id #uuid "eb14b59d-3f60-5be8-9b8f-51c1297ee0f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123967} {:answer/id #uuid "0e4d25c4-8d53-58fe-8719-4a9708aef584" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123968} {:answer/id #uuid "571c71e7-04c9-571d-b307-95bec7375fb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94b3a172-3584-5ffd-982f-0f3370f6e593" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123965} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e581fc2-2f3c-59f7-aa50-f3b3371d4f0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123964}] :question/id #uuid "1d4d76d9-0c38-5edc-a8b9-c85d1bae32b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94694" :question/problem "Let $U$ be the set of polynomials of the form $ax^{3}$, where $a ∈ R$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $h(x) ∈ U$, where $h(x) = 3x$.\n2. For any $p(x) ∈ U$ and $k ∈ R$, we have $k \\cdot p(x) ∈ U$.\n3. The set is closed under scalar multiplication."}]}
